  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p>On June 11, Trump cancelled planned airstrikes against Iran and posted on Truth Social that Iran's Supreme Leader had approved a draft framework for a 60-day ceasefire extension followed by a 60-day nuclear negotiation window, with a possible signing "in Europe" — consistent with the G7 summit in Evian (June 15–17). Behind the scenes, FM Araghchi and Qatari envoy Ali Al-Thawadi worked through the night of June 11–12 in Tehran. Iran's MFA spokesman Baghaei told IRNA and Reuters that "most of the text had been finalized but the Americans kept changing their positions" and that Tehran had reached "no final decision." Trump's announcement is his 39th-plus claim of an imminent Iran deal since March 23; the gap between US public framing and Iranian official confirmation remains the central analytical problem. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span>, drove <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>The IRGC issued a formal written closure declaration for the Strait of Hormuz on June 11, threatening to target any vessel attempting transit. PortWatch finalized 2 commercial transits on June 7 (baseline: ~94/day); the 7-day moving average remains in single digits. The closure has now entered its 105th day. European allies (UK, France, 15+ nations) bring an operationally-ready demining plan to the G7 in Evian June 15–17, contingent on Trump's endorsement and a finalized peace deal. Congress was told in April that mine-clearing could take six months even after operations commence. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>On June 7–8, Israel and Iran traded the worst strikes since the April ceasefire: the IRGC launched ballistic missiles at Ramat David Airbase (IDF confirmed damage); the IDF struck military and petrochemical targets in western and central Iran, including a facility at Mahshahr used to produce materials for Iran's ballistic missile program. Netanyahu announced a halt "for now" while stating Israel is "prepared to return to war." IDF Chief of Staff said Israel had planned a "much more significant and severe blow" — a public statement of restrained escalation. Iran likewise suspended further strikes but warned of resumption if Israeli operations in southern Lebanon continued. As of June 12 no new bilateral exchange has been confirmed post-June 8. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>The IAEA Board of Governors on June 10 adopted a resolution (21–3–10) expressing "grave concern" over Iran's non-compliance — the most severe Board language since 2005. DG Grossi's June 4 report confirmed 97 days of zero inspector access to Fordow, Natanz, and Isfahan and an unverifiable 440.9 kg HEU stockpile. On June 12, the Board formally declared Iran non-compliant with its NPT Comprehensive Safeguards Agreement for the first time in 20 years. The Quad (US, UK, France, Germany) submitted a joint statement; Russia and China voted against. This IAEA ruling adds a new international legal dimension to any framework deal and gives hardliners in Tehran a pretext to demand that the Board ruling be withdrawn as a precondition for agreement. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:38%;">Escalation 38%</div>
